WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.645
Internal network abuse occurs when a user-controlled URL or destination influences a server-side request.

2
00:00:06.645 --> 00:00:14.867
Features such as URL previews, webhook testing, file import, and document conversion can become SSRF entry points.

3
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:18.818
Cloud metadata exploitation is a major SSRF scenario.

4
00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:26.680
If a workload can reach metadata endpoints, attackers may attempt to obtain temporary credentials or environmental information.

5
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:32.228
Trust boundary violations happen when applications treat user-supplied destinations as safe.

6
00:00:32.228 --> 00:00:39.133
URL validation, protocol restrictions, redirect handling, and DNS behavior need careful design.

7
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:51.136
SSRF defenses work best when application validation is paired with network segmentation, egress filtering, infrastructure protections, and monitoring for unusual server-initiated traffic.

8
00:00:51.136 --> 00:00:54.658
Use network segmentation and infrastructure protections.

9
00:00:54.658 --> 00:00:59.128
Validate URLs and restrict destinations with allow lists where possible.

10
00:00:59.128 --> 00:01:03.508
Apply egress filtering and monitor unusual outbound requests.

11
00:01:03.508 --> 00:01:07.599
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC6.

12
00:01:07.599 --> 00:01:11.112
6 - Network security protections; CC7.

13
00:01:11.112 --> 00:01:13.716
1 - Threat and vulnerability management.
